 "topic": "why too large a learning rate makes gradient descent diverge",
 "subject": "computing",
 "hook_type": "wrong-answer reveal",
 "caption": "On f(x) = x², each gradient descent step multiplies x by (1 − 2η). Step size η = 0.1 creeps, η = 0.5 lands in one step, and any η above 1 overshoots further every time. Curvature sets the speed limit: η < 2/f″.\n\nShare this with someone tuning a learning rate right now.\n\n#machinelearning #gradientdescent #deeplearning #optimization #ai #computerscience #math #stem",
 "cover_at": 1.0,
 "hook": "Bigger steps should reach the bottom faster. They don't.",
 "sub": "Gradient descent, in one picture.",
 "beats": [
  {
   "text": "Gradient descent finds the bottom of a valley by stepping against the slope, scaled by a step size $\\eta$.",
   "do": [
